# A SETBACK AND A GAP, SIDE BY SIDE, DRAWN WITH THE SAME GLYPH.
#
# The fixture for M22's parapet rule. Both shapes below are runs of `_` -- the
# format has no way to say which is which and should not gain one -- so the rule
# has to tell them apart by GEOMETRY, and this file is the case that proves it
# does.
#
#   ROWS 2-8, COLUMNS 0-2 AND 13-14: a SETBACK. The void runs unbroken off the
#   side of the canvas, so this is the bridge being narrower here. Column 3 and
#   column 12 are its edges and must grow a parapet, or a narrow section is an
#   unrailed drop -- which is what every generated `narrow` stretch in the game
#   was until this rule existed.
#
#   ROWS 4-6, COLUMNS 7-8: a GAP. Deck closes around it on both sides, so it is
#   broken structure rather than a railed balcony. It must stay unrailed: you are
#   supposed to be able to fall into it, and to shove a stone through it.
#
# THE TWO ARE DELIBERATELY OVERLAPPING IN Z. A rule that keyed off "is this row
# narrowed" rather than off the void's own shape would rail the gap too on rows
# 4-6, and a fixture that separated them could never catch that.
#
# Entry and exit rows are solid across so the segment validates and so a run can
# join it, which is the invariant M22 leans on for the join contract.

name = test_setback
base_height = 0
width = 15
length = 12
tags = fixture, setback

[deck]
...............
...............
___..........__
___..........__
___....__....__
___....__....__
___....__....__
___..........__
___..........__
